92186572660 1079 #uuid "f835e1bd-3b54-55df-a86e-257a12043f7d" 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572661 1075 17592186572358 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572661 1075 17592186572476 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572661 1075 17592186572480 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572661 1075 17592186572529 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572661 1075 17592186572543 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572661 1076 17592186045493 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572661 1077 "selection" 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572661 1078 17592186572476 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572661 1079 #uuid "f9852f19-1bc8-5b08-8e67-b849c3edbd0c" 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572662 1068 17592186572588 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572662 1069 "q-75163" 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572662 1070 17592186045487 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572662 1071 "Given that $x+3$ is a factor of $f(x)=-{x}^{3}-7{x}^{2}-15x-9$, which of the following curves is generated by $y=f(x)?$\n\n*(Note: Figures not drawn to scale)*" 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572662 1072 #uuid "024af745-75a4-59f6-8f6d-ac762eef1195" 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572662 1074 "First, we need to factor the polynomial. We are told that $(x+3)$ is a factor of $f(x),$ so $(x+3)$ divides $f(x)$ with zero remainder. To begin factoring $f(x),$ we can divide it by $(x+3)$ using synthetic division:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & {x}^{3} & {x}^{2} & {x}^{1} & {x}^{0} \\\\ -3 & -1 & -7 & -15 & -9 \\\\  &  & 3 & 12 & 9 \\\\  & -1 & -4 & -3 & 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{f(x)}{x+3} & =-{x}^{2}-4x-3 \\\\  & =-({x}^{2}+4x+3)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\nf(x)=-(x+3)({x}^{2}+4x+3).\n$$\n\nWe can factor the quadratic factor even further, and get\n\n$$\nf(x)=-(x+3)(x+1)(x+3)=-(x+1)(x+3)^{2}\n$$\n\nNow that we have the cubic in factored form, we can tell that it has a single root at $x=-1$ and a double root at $x=-3.$\n\nWe also find the $y$-intercept of the curve by substituting the value $x=0$ into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-(0+1)(0+3)^{2} \\\\  & =-(1)(3)^{2} \\\\  & =-9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the curve\n\n- intercepts the $y$-axis at $(0,-9),$\n\n- passes through the $x$-axis at the single root $x=-1,$ and\n\n- touches the $x$-axis and turns around at the double root $x=-3.$\n\nBecause the leading coefficient is negative, the graph takes an $\\text{S}$-shape. Therefore, the graph looks as follows:\n\n![](images/c0/c084c0059cecb1fa33430bc3761a4e5fbe23a44f3db59d61b9f2acfc74a87fa7.png)" 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572663 1068 17592186572624 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572663 1069 "q-83019" 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572663 1070 17592186045486 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572663 1071 "Simplify $(A∨B)∨(C∨B).$" 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572663 1072 #uuid "0268bb49-20bf-526d-b894-f6feb846e8a0" 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572663 1074 "Using the associative, commutative, and idempotent laws, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(A∨B)∨(C∨B) & ≡(A∨B)∨(B∨C) \\\\  & ≡A∨(B∨B)∨C \\\\  & ≡A∨B∨C \\\\  & ≡A∨C∨B \\\\  & ≡(A∨C)∨B.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572664 1068 17592186572625 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572664 1069 "q-280320" 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572664 1070 17592186045486 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572664 1071 "Calculate the volume $V$ of the parallelepiped spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{AB},$ $\\overset{\\to }{AC},$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AD},$ given the points $A(0,2,-1),$ $B(3,4,1),$ $C(-1,3,2),$ and $D(2,1,0).$\n\n$V=${{field-1}}" 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572664 1072 #uuid "02e4dd76-291f-501d-9dd9-df687e45b52b" 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572664 1074 "First, we find the displacement vectors $\\overset{\\to }{AB},$ $\\overset{\\to }{AC},$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AD}$ that span the parallelepiped:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =b-a \\\\  & =(3i+4j+k)-(2j-k) \\\\  & =3i+2j+2k \\\\  & =⟨3,2,2⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =c-a \\\\  & =(-i+3j+2k)-(2j-k) \\\\  & =-i+j+3k \\\\  & =⟨-1,1,3⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{AD} & =d-a \\\\  & =(2i+j)-(2j-k) \\\\  & =2i-j+k \\\\  & =⟨2,-1,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to find the volume of the parallelepiped, we need to take the absolute value of the triple product of the above vectors.\n\nComputing the triple product, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\cdot (\\overset{\\to }{AC}\\times \\overset{\\to }{AD}) & =\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 2 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =3\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{vmatrix}-2\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{vmatrix}+2\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =3(1\\cdot 1-3\\cdot (-1))-2((-1)\\cdot 1-3\\cdot 2)+2((-1)\\cdot (-1)-1\\cdot 2) \\\\  & =3(1+3)-2(-1-6)+2(1-2) \\\\  & =12-(-14)-2 \\\\  & =24.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the volume of the corresponding parallelpiped spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{AB},$ $\\overset{\\to }{AC},$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AD}$ is\n\n$$\nV=|24|=24.\n$$" 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572665 1068 17592186572643 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572665 1069 "q-67981" 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572665 1070 17592186045485 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572665 1071 "Evaluate ${lim}_{x\\to -1}\\frac{{x}^{2}-2x-3}{{x}^{2}-1}.$" 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572665 1072 #uuid "0496054d-648c-503a-af82-71aac17fd7ca" 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572665 1074 "If we attempt to evaluate the limit, we get an indeterminate form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -1}\\frac{{x}^{2}-2x-3}{{x}^{2}-1} & =\\frac{(-1)^{2}-2(-1)-3}{(-1)^{2}-1}=\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, to find the limit, we have to factor the numerator and the denominator. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}-2x-3}{{x}^{2}-1} & =\\frac{(x+1)(x-3)}{(x-1)(x+1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{x-3}{x-1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -1}\\frac{{x}^{2}-2x-3}{{x}^{2}-1} & ={lim}_{x\\to -1}\\frac{x-3}{x-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{-1-3}{-1-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{-4}{-2} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572666 1068 17592186572647 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572666 1069 "q-120247" 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572666 1070 17592186045487 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572666 1071 "![](images/2e/2ea6acbf6574e970a798100729de9a92874bca90b8153db9883daa1cd6d98c2a.png)\n\nConsider the picture above. Given that $c=\\lambda a+2b$, find $\\lambda .$" 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572666 1072 #uuid "049a751e-712d-54cc-ad6d-c6e3564444d6" 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572666 1074 "To find $\\lambda ,$ we want to match the expression\n\n$$\nc=\\lambda a+2b.\n$$\n\nWe are given that one of the components is $2b.$ So, we first draw the vector $2b$ from the origin. Then we complete the path to the point $C.$\n\n![](images/3c/3c31e9b0d926de6a3f8c9f35bd816349d6488c8b98864d8576fdbfc395bbd92d.png)\n\nLet $P$ be the endpoint of the vector $2b.$ Then\n\n$$\nc=\\overset{\\to }{OP}+\\overset{\\to }{PC}.\n$$\n\nIn $△OPC,$ we have:\n\n- $PC=3|a|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{PC}$ and $a$ have the same direction\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{PC}=3a.$\n\nAdditionally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c & =\\overset{\\to }{OP}+\\overset{\\to }{PC} \\\\  & =2b+3a.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $c=\\lambda a+2b=3a+2b.$\n\nSince $a∦b,$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients of $a,$ we get:\n\n$$\n\\lambda =3\n$$" 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572667 1068 17592186572601 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572667 1069 "q-157235" 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572667 1070 17592186045487 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572667 1071 "Consider the following vectors:\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix},\\,{a}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -5 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix},\\,{a}_{3}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -7 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $3{a}_{1}-{a}_{2}+2{a}_{3}=0.$\n\nII. ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3}=0$ only if ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}=0.$\n\nIII. The set $\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}\\}$ is linearly dependent." 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572667 1072 #uuid "0bcf3acb-d9f4-5535-b8d1-de8e7ee2e9d3" 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572667 1074 "We say that three vectors ${v}_{1},{v}_{2}$ and ${v}_{3}$ are *linearly independent* if the only solution to the equation\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{v}_{1}+{x}_{2}{v}_{2}+{x}_{3}{v}_{3}=0\n$$\n\nis ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}=0.$ Otherwise, the vectors are *linearly dependent*.\n\nWith that in mind, let's look at each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{a}_{1}-{a}_{2}+2{a}_{3} & =3\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -5 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}+2\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -7 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ 9 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -5 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ -14 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}6-4-2 \\\\ 9+5-14 \\\\ -3+3+0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Since statement I is true, we have ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3}=0$ for ${x}_{1}=3,\\,{x}_{2}=-1$ and ${x}_{3}=2.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since statement II is false, the set $\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}\\}$ is linearly *dependent*.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572668 1068 17592186572637 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572668 1069 "q-28194" 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572668 1070 17592186045487 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572668 1071 "Given that $(x+1)$ is a factor of $f(x)=-{x}^{3}-3{x}^{2}-4x-2,$ which of the following curves is generated by $y=f(x)?$\n\n(*Note: Figures not drawn to scale*)" 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572668 1072 #uuid "0d6ccda5-f064-5d51-bbf4-385a52f598a9" 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572668 1074 "First, we need to factor the polynomial. We are told that $(x+1)$ is a factor of $f(x),$ so $(x+1)$ divides $f(x)$ with zero remainder. To begin factoring $f(x),$ we can divide it by $(x+1)$ using synthetic division:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & {x}^{3} & {x}^{2} & {x}^{1} & {x}^{0} \\\\ -1 & -1 & -3 & -4 & -2 \\\\  &  & 1 & 2 & 2 \\\\  & -1 & -2 & -2 & 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{f(x)}{x+1} & =-{x}^{2}-2x-2 \\\\  & =-({x}^{2}+2x+2),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\nf(x)=-(x+1)({x}^{2}+2x+2).\n$$\n\nThe equation is now fully factored. Note that the discriminant of ${x}^{2}+2x+2=0$ is \n\n$$\nD={2}^{2}-4(1)(2)=-4<0,\n$$\n\nwhich implies that the quadratic factor has no real roots. So by the zero-product rule, we deduce that $f(x)$ has a single root at $x=-1.$ \n\nWe also find the $y$-intercept of the curve by substituting the value $x=0$ into the equation:\n\n$$\ny=-(0)^{3}-3(0)^{2}-4(0)-2=-2\n$$\n\nSo, the curve:\n\n- has a $y$-intercept at $(0,-2),$\n\n- passes through the $x$-axis at the single root $x=-1,$ and\n\n- has no more $x$-intercepts.\n\nBecause the leading coefficient is negative, the graph has an $\\text{S}$-shape. Therefore, the graph looks as follows:\n\n![](images/de/de0741fcb960e2b140660ac01307c8d6e69c3998fb79233ba8d17755c3d10fc0.png)" 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572669 1068 17592186572620 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572669 1069 "q-81720" 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572669 1070 17592186045485 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572669 1071 "Which of the following are logically equivalent to $Y?$\n\nI. $(Z∧Y)∨Y$\n\nII. $Y∧(Z∧Y)$\n\nIII. $Y∨(Y∨Z)$" 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572669 1072 #uuid "0fee1624-769e-58a6-a8e5-0e952d79cac1" 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572669 1074 "The law that disjunction absorbs conjunction is stated as\n\n$$\nY∨(Y∧Z)≡Y.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each of the given statements.\n\n- Statement I is logically equivalent to $Y.$ We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(Z∧Y)∨Y & ≡Y∨(Z∧Y) \\\\  & ≡Y∨(Y∧Z) \\\\  & ≡Y.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is *not* logically equivalent to $Y.$ This statement cannot be simplified using the absorption laws.\n\n- Statement III is *not* logically equivalent to $Y.$ This statement cannot be simplified using the absorption laws.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572670 1068 17592186572653 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572670 1069 "q-340339" 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572670 1070 17592186045485 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572670 1071 "Given that $y=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{2}^{x}},$ find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.$\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{x}{{2}^{x}}\\cdot ($\n{{field-1}}\n$).$" 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572670 1072 #uuid "11dd8eeb-96f6-50fa-a820-1e22e1912967" 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572670 1074 "Let $u={x}^{2}$ and $v={2}^{x}.$ Then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} & =2x\\,\\text{and}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}={2}^{x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the quotient rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}-u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}}{{v}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{2}^{x}\\cdot 2x-{x}^{2}\\cdot {2}^{x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{{({2}^{x})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{2}^{x}\\cdot x(2-x\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)}{{({2}^{x})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{x(2-x\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)}{{2}^{x}} \\\\  & =\\frac{x}{{2}^{x}}\\cdot (\\,2-x\\operatorname{ln}⁡2\\,).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572671 1068 17592186572644 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572671 1069 "q-95398" 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572671 1070 17592186045486 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572671 1071 "Consider the change of variables given by the matrix equation\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{y}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nand the curve $C,$ defined as\n\n$$\nC:\\,{x}_{1}^{2}+4{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}+2{x}_{1}-2{x}_{2}=2.\n$$\n\nGiven that the change of variables above reduces $Q(x)={x}_{1}^{2}+4{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}$ to its canonical (diagonal) form $3{y}_{1}^{2}-{y}_{2}^{2},$ what type of a quadratic curve is defined by $C?$" 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572671 1072 #uuid "13426e81-09a1-5f21-879b-4654e3684393" 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572671 1074 "First, we note the following:\n\n$$\nC:\\,{{{x}_{1}^{2}+4{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}}_{⏟}}_{Q(x)}+2{x}_{1}-2{x}_{2}=2\n$$\n\nLet's write the given change of variables explicitly:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{y}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}\\,⟹\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} &=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}{y}_{1}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}{y}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2} &=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}{y}_{1}+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}{y}_{2},\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe know that the given change of variables reduces $Q(x)$ to its canonical form. Therefore, performing the change of variables on $C,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{2}+4{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}+2{x}_{1}-2{x}_{2} & =2 \\\\ 3{y}_{1}^{2}-{y}_{2}^{2}+2(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}{y}_{1}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}{y}_{2})-2(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}{y}_{1}+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}{y}_{2}) & =2 \\\\ 3{y}_{1}^{2}-{y}_{2}^{2}-\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{2}}{y}_{2} & =2 \\\\ 3{y}_{1}^{2}-{y}_{2}^{2}-2\\sqrt{2}{y}_{2} & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we complete the square for the variable ${y}_{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{y}_{1}^{2}-{y}_{2}^{2}-2\\sqrt{2}{y}_{2} & =2 \\\\ 3{y}_{1}^{2}-({y}_{2}^{2}+2\\sqrt{2}{y}_{2}) & =2 \\\\ 3{y}_{1}^{2}-({y}_{2}^{2}+2\\sqrt{2}{y}_{2}+(\\sqrt{2})^{2}-(\\sqrt{2})^{2}) & =2 \\\\ 3{y}_{1}^{2}-({y}_{2}^{2}+2\\sqrt{2}{y}_{2}+2-2) & =2 \\\\ 3{y}_{1}^{2}-{({y}_{2}+\\sqrt{2})}^{2}+2 & =2 \\\\ 3{y}_{1}^{2}-{({y}_{2}+\\sqrt{2})}^{2} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, performing the change of variables\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{z}_{1} &={y}_{1}, \\\\ {z}_{2} &={y}_{2}+\\sqrt{2},\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nwe get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{z}_{1}^{2}-{z}_{2}^{2} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis represents the pair of intersecting lines ${z}_{2}=\\sqrt{3}{z}_{1}$ and ${z}_{2}=-\\sqrt{3}{z}_{1}.$" 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572672 1068 17592186572596 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572672 1069 "q-340304" 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572672 1070 17592186045485 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572672 1071 "Given that $f(x)=\\frac{x}{1-{x}^{2}}$, find ${f}^{′}(x).$\n\n${f}^{′}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572672 1072 #uuid "17245a50-7006-5425-919f-edc58b5da5cf" 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572672 1074 "The numerator and denominator are $u(x)=x$ and $v(x)=1-{x}^{2},$ respectively. Their derivatives are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′}(x) & =1, \\\\ {v}^{′}(x) & =-2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the quotient rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{{u}^{′}(x)\\cdot v(x)-u(x)\\cdot {v}^{′}(x)}{[v(x)]^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1\\cdot (1-{x}^{2})-x\\cdot (-2x)}{{(1-{x}^{2})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1-{x}^{2}+2{x}^{2}}{{(1-{x}^{2})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1+{x}^{2}}{{(1-{x}^{2})}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572673 1068 17592186572645 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572673 1069 "q-340288" 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572673 1070 17592186045485 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572673 1071 "Given that $f(x)=\\frac{-3}{4x-5}$, find ${f}^{′}(x).$\n\n${f}^{′}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572673 1072 #uuid "1cf3cdc5-a966-53dd-81de-da3c76897f3e" 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572673 1074 "The numerator and denominator are $u(x)=-3$ and $v(x)=4x-5,$ respectively. Their derivatives are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′}(x) & =0, \\\\ {v}^{′}(x) & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the quotient rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{{u}^{′}(x)\\cdot v(x)-u(x)\\cdot {v}^{′}(x)}{[v(x)]^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{0\\cdot (4x-5)-(-3)\\cdot 4}{{(4x-5)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{12}{{(4x-5)}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572674 1068 17592186572627 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572674 1069 "q-32562" 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572674 1070 17592186045487 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572674 1071 "Solve for $x$ if ${log}_{3}⁡(x-5)=2.$" 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572674 1072 #uuid "1eda43e3-b2a8-5d39-8cf8-7675e676743f" 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572674 1074 "To remove the logarithm, we exponentiate both sides of the equation. The base of the logarithm is $3,$ so we use this base for the exponentiation as well:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{3}⁡(x-5) & =2 \\\\ {3}^{{log}_{3}⁡(x-5)} & ={3}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since exponentiation is the inverse function of the logarithm, we get ${3}^{{log}_{3}⁡(x-5)}=x-5.$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-5 & ={3}^{2} \\\\ x-5 & =9 \\\\ x & =14.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572675 1068 17592186572592 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572675 1069 "q-88260" 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572675 1070 17592186045485 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572675 1071 "Write $\\frac{4x-1}{4x-7}$ in the form $a+\\frac{b}{4x-7},$ where $a\\ne 0.$ What is the value of $a+b?$" 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572675 1072 #uuid "1f4665bd-3b2f-5e28-98c1-a2915a1c6960" 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572675 1074 "First, we subtract and add $7$ in the numerator. This reveals a copy of the denominator without changing the value of the expression.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4x-1}{4x-7} &=\\frac{4x-7+7-1}{4x-7}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we split the fraction into two, and then simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4x-7+7-1}{4x-7} & =\\frac{4x-7}{4x-7}+\\frac{7-1}{4x-7} \\\\  & =1+\\frac{6}{4x-7}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have $a=1$ and $b=6,$ and we get\n\n$$\na+b=1+6=7.\n$$" 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572676 1068 17592186572656 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572676 1069 "q-83007" 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572676 1070 17592186045485 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572676 1071 "Identify the sentence that is equivalent to the following: \"The juice is strawberry or it is orange.\"" 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572676 1072 #uuid "1fc90f62-3260-577e-8311-14eaab4c330e" 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572676 1074 "First, let's write the sentence more concisely:\n\n\"The juice is strawberry or orange.\"\n\nUsing the commutative law, we can swap the order of the words \"strawberry\" and \"orange.\" The sentence now reads\n\n\"The juice is orange or strawberry.\"" 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572677 1068 17592186572654 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572677 1069 "q-119896" 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572677 1070 17592186045487 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572677 1071 "![](images/21/212a339585b98f156d68f2e4260c0acbb474c51782b6d72acf0b89fd5c1fe69e.png)\n\nGiven the vectors $a$ and $b$ shown above, which of the following could be the linear combination $2a-2b?$" 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572677 1072 #uuid "22749eae-3c2e-573a-a168-cb568838d8f3" 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572677 1074 "First, recall that\n\n$$\n2a-2b=2a+(-2b).\n$$\n\n- The vector $2a$ has the same direction as $a$ but twice the magnitude.\n\n- The vector $-2b$ has the direction that is opposite to $b$ but twice the magnitude.\n\n![](images/c1/c1cc845ddfe16f12169625e5f8404d3d92558b885ffde8c3ab02cdb2c096f699.png)\n\nNow, we can draw $-2b$ starting from the head of $2a.$ The resultant \n\n$$\n2a-2b\n$$\n\ngoes from the tail of $2a$ to the head of $-2b.$\n\n![](images/5d/5d22db7e37ff1c5b55f1ec595da919f987a6c1fea4247cf5a075f27c198ba545.png)" 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572678 1068 17592186572636 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572678 1068 17592186572649 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572678 1069 "q-177678" 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572678 1070 17592186045485 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572678 1071 "The solution to the inequality $-10x-13<17$ is$\\,x$\n{{field-1}}{{field-2}}." 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572678 1072 #uuid "24db3001-b38e-58e0-a2db-d37c5cfbbf6a" 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572678 1074 "First, we apply the addition principle, adding $13$ to both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-10x-13 & <17 \\\\ -10x-13+13 & <17+13 \\\\ -10x+0 & <30 \\\\ -10x & <30\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we apply the multiplication principle, dividing both sides by $-10.$ \n\nRemember, when multiplying or dividing an inequality by a negative number, we need to flip the inequality sign:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-10x\\,\\, & <\\,\\,30 \\\\ \\frac{-10x}{-10}\\, & >\\,\\,\\frac{30}{-10} \\\\ \\frac{\\cancel{-10}x}{\\cancel{-10}}\\, & >\\,\\,-3 \\\\ x\\,\\, & >\\,-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x>-3.$" 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572679 1068 17592186572616 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572679 1068 17592186572648 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572679 1069 "q-177686" 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572679 1070 17592186045485 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572679 1071 "The solution to the inequality $2<9y-7$ is $y${{field-1}}{{field-2}}." 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572679 1072 #uuid "24f35f07-8a41-5d2b-b978-5fe26a8e2dc9" 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572679 1074 "First, we apply the addition principle, adding $7$ to both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2 & <9y-7 \\\\ 2+7 & <9y-7+7 \\\\ 9 & <9y+0 \\\\ 9 & <9y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSecond, we apply the multiplication principle, dividing both sides by $9:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9 & <9y \\\\ \\frac{9}{9} & <\\frac{9y}{9} \\\\ 1 & <\\frac{\\cancel{9}y}{\\cancel{9}} \\\\ 1 & <y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, let's swap the left and right-hand sides so that the variable is on the left-hand side. Remember, we also need to flip the inequality.\n\n$$\ny>1\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $y>1.$" 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572680 1068 17592186572590 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572680 1068 17592186572602 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572680 1069 "q-320734" 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572680 1070 17592186045487 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572680 1071 "Write $\\frac{3{x}^{6}+8}{3{x}^{6}+2x}$ in the form $a+\\frac{bx+c}{3{x}^{6}+2x},$ where $a\\ne 0.$\n\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"1\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"25\" width=\"20\">$3{x}^{6}+8$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" rowspan=\"2\" width=\"20\">$=$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" rowspan=\"2\" width=\"20\">\n{{field-1}}\n</td>\n<td align=\"center\" rowspan=\"2\" width=\"20\">$+$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"25\" width=\"20\">\n{{field-2}} \n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"25\" width=\"20\">$3{x}^{6}+2x$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"25\" width=\"25\">$3{x}^{6}+2x$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572680 1072 #uuid "2560be5d-dea1-5429-b927-2f169d6e2bc3" 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572680 1074 "First, we add and subtract $2x$ in the numerator. This reveals a copy of the denominator without changing the value of the expression.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3{x}^{6}+8}{3{x}^{6}+2x}\\, &=\\,\\frac{3{x}^{6}+2x-2x+8}{3{x}^{6}+2x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we split the fraction into two, and then simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3{x}^{6}+2x-2x+8}{3{x}^{6}+2x} & =\\frac{3{x}^{6}+2x}{3{x}^{4}+2x}+\\frac{-2x+8}{3{x}^{6}+2x} \\\\  & =1+\\frac{-2x+8}{3{x}^{6}+2x}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572681 1068 17592186572630 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572681 1069 "q-77510" 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572681 1070 17592186045486 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572681 1071 "![](images/f2/f2e79d1a16c7bba471bed8a443673449c7398486d19a84e12a91ca514c793329.png)\n\nGiven the vectors $p$ and $q$ shown above, which of the following could be the linear combination $-2p-4q?$" 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572681 1072 #uuid "2a129def-6612-5e6f-9d02-8b4f3371bc09" 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572681 1074 "First, recall that\n\n$$\n-2p-4q=-2p+(-4q).\n$$\n\n- The vector $-2p$ has the direction that is opposite to $p$ and $2$ times the magnitude.\n\n- The vector $-4q$ has the direction that is opposite to $q$ and $4$ times the magnitude.\n\n![](images/de/de82fa1937ae9f7ccb1b739cc8e6ea9848a71ea216df600917706717711aa081.png)\n\nNow, we can draw $-4q$ starting from the head of $-2p.$ The resultant \n\n$$\n-2p-4q\n$$\n\ngoes from the tail of $-2p$ to the head of $-4q.$\n\n![](images/2d/2d388be7761bd2fbae0593b8e715f1873e4170e577132b4a6838412e3629c480.png)" 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572682 1068 17592186572650 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572682 1069 "q-79388" 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572682 1070 17592186045487 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572682 1071 "If $7{log}_{5}⁡x-2{log}_{5}⁡x=10$, then what is the value of  $x?$" 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572682 1072 #uuid "2f3b2436-4186-5dc4-8dde-9327a375de0e" 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572682 1074 "First, we rearrange our equation to isolate the logarithmic term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7{log}_{5}⁡x-2{log}_{5}⁡x & =10 \\\\ (7-2){log}_{5}⁡x & =10 \\\\ 5{log}_{5}⁡x & =10 \\\\ {log}_{5}⁡x & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo remove the logarithm, we exponentiate both sides of the equation. The base of the logarithm is $5,$ so we use this base for the exponentiation as well:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{5}⁡x & =2 \\\\ {5}^{{log}_{5}⁡x} & ={5}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since exponentiation is the inverse function of the logarithm, we get ${5}^{{log}_{5}⁡x}=x.$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={5}^{2} \\\\ x & =25.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572683 1068 17592186572658 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572683 1069 "q-340313" 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572683 1070 17592186045485 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572683 1071 "If $f(x)=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{{5}^{x}}$,  find ${f}^{′}(x).$\n\n${f}^{′}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572683 1072 #uuid "32d1ab28-930b-52ce-95a3-03b4df4fad2a" 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572683 1074 "In this case, the expression for $f(x)$ is a quotient $\\frac{u(x)}{v(x)}$ with $u(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ and $v(x)={5}^{x}.$ \n\nTherefore, we apply the quotient rule as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{{u}^{′}(x)\\cdot v(x)-u(x)\\cdot {v}^{′}(x)}{[v(x)]^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)^{′}\\cdot {5}^{x}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\cdot ({5}^{x})^{′}}{{({5}^{x})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\cdot {5}^{x}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\cdot {5}^{x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}{{({5}^{x})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{5}^{x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{ln}⁡5)}{{({5}^{x})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}{{5}^{x}}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572684 1068 17592186572629 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572684 1069 "q-18652" 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572684 1070 17592186045487 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572684 1071 "![](images/36/3699487cf2de2ca4e996fb57f46e167495c8a118a4b4a5421e665e2bb7f84eff.png)\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is defined and differentiable on $[-3,4].$ The graph of ${f}^{′}(x),$ the derivative of $f(x),$ is shown above. Find the maximum value of $f(x)$ on the interval $[-3,4]$ given that $f(-3)=4.$" 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572684 1072 #uuid "3a7ee813-eddb-55cf-91b1-fd20b615ae7e" 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572684 1074 "According to the candidate test, the maximum value of $f(x)$ on $[-3,4]$ must occur at a critical point or an endpoint of the interval. \n\n**Step 1**: Find the critical points:\n\nWe see that ${f}^{′}(x)$ is defined everywhere on $x\\in (-3,4),$ and ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ at $x=0.$ Therefore, the candidates are $x=-3,0,4.$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the value of $f$ for each candidate using the fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\nThe function ${f}^{′}(x)$ is made up of $2$ trapezoids. Let's add the areas bounded by these shapes to our diagram:\n\n![](images/8a/8a7521b862c6533554df3931b03f7aa4b6b669167ced130e4bb353605fd24ea2.png)\n\nUsing the given information and the fundamental theorem of calculus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-3) & =4 \\\\ f(0) & =f(-3)+{\\int }_{-3}^{0}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4-4 \\\\  & =0 \\\\ f(4) & =f(-3)+{\\int }_{-3}^{4}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4-4+6 \\\\  & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Select the largest value of $f$ from our candidates. \n\nComparing the values of $f$ found in step $2,$ we have\n\n$$\n{f}_{\\text{max}}=max\\{4,0,6\\}=6.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the maximum value of $f$ is ${f}_{\\text{max}}=6,$ and the value of $x$ that maximizes $f(x)$ is ${x}_{\\text{max}}=4.$" 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572685 1068 17592186572621 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572685 1069 "q-86780" 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572685 1070 17592186045487 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572685 1071 "Which of the following are logically equivalent to $H?$\n\nI. $(¬K∧H)∨H$\n\nII. $(H∨¬K)∨H$\n\nIII. $H∨(¬K∧H)$" 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572685 1072 #uuid "3b3dcd41-a4d5-5b14-ab14-5e50dc512f91" 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572685 1074 "The law that disjunction absorbs conjunction is stated as\n\n$$\nH∨(H∧¬K)≡H.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each of the given statements.\n\n- Statement I is logically equivalent to $H.$ We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(¬K∧H)∨H & ≡H∨(¬K∧H) \\\\  & ≡H∨(H∧¬K) \\\\  & ≡H.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is *not* logically equivalent to $H.$ This statement cannot be simplified using the absorption laws.\n\n- Statement III is logically equivalent to $H.$ We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}H∨(¬K∧H) & ≡H∨(H∧¬K) \\\\  & ≡H.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572686 1068 17592186572626 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572686 1069 "q-42880" 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572686 1070 17592186045487 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572686 1071 "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 10 & 2 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix given above. Identify **all** of the reasons why one can conclude that $|A|=0?$\n\nI. $A$ has proportional rows\n\nII. $A$ has proportional columns\n\nIII. $A$ has identical rows" 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572686 1072 #uuid "3e928130-63ab-538e-924b-5fd72d363a24" 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572686 1074 "Let's examine each of the statements in turn.\n\n- $A$ has proportional rows: \n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 10 & 2 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\\,✓\n$$\n\n- $A$ does *not* have proportional columns.\n\n- $A$ does *not* have identical rows.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572687 1068 17592186572639 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572687 1069 "q-127457" 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572687 1070 17592186045487 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572687 1071 "Find the Cartesian equation for the line with the following vector equation:\n\n$$\nr=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}+t\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix},\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )\n$$" 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572687 1072 #uuid "3f6ecd28-8af0-5770-801c-e2211846f047" 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572687 1074 "Using the usual formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-(-1)}{0}=\\frac{y-2}{5}=\\frac{z-4}{7}=t,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),\n$$\n\nor equivalently\n\n$$\n\\frac{x+1}{0}=\\frac{y-2}{5}=\\frac{z-4}{7},\n$$\n\nwhich is problematic because $\\frac{x+1}{0}$ involves division by zero.\n\nHowever, notice that in our line, the $x$ component always maintains the constant value of $x=-1:$\n\n$$\nr=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2+5t \\\\ 4+7t\\end{bmatrix},\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )\n$$\n\nSo, in the Cartesian equation, we remove the $\\frac{x+1}{0}$ part and instead write $x=-1$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{y-2}{5}=\\frac{z-4}{7},\\,x=-1\n$$" 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572688 1068 17592186572640 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572688 1069 "q-89268" 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572688 1070 17592186045487 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572688 1071 "Simplify $(M∧N)∧(N∨N).$" 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572688 1072 #uuid "40635742-9288-5d2d-bfdc-3674acf9908b" 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572688 1074 "Using the associative and idempotent laws, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(M∧N)∧(N∨N) & ≡(M∧N)∧N \\\\  & ≡M∧(N∧N) \\\\  & ≡M∧N.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572689 1068 17592186572604 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572689 1069 "q-280715" 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572689 1070 17592186045485 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572689 1071 "Compute the volume $V$ of the parallelepiped spanned by the vectors $p=⟨1,0,5⟩$, $q=⟨3,2,6⟩$, and $r=⟨0,4,1⟩.$\n\n$V=${{field-1}}" 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572689 1072 #uuid "443a4021-2ecb-5b8a-948d-b71804528fab" 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572689 1074 "The volume of a parallelepiped spanned by three vectors is given by the absolute value of their triple product.\n\nFirst, we compute the triple product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p\\cdot (q\\times r) & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 & 5 \\\\ 3 & 2 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}2 & 6 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{vmatrix}-0\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}3 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix}+5\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1(2\\cdot 1-6\\cdot 4)+0+5(3\\cdot 4-2\\cdot 0) \\\\  & =-22+60 \\\\  & =38\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we take the absolute value. So, the volume of the corresponding parallelepiped is \n\n$$\nV=|38|=38.\n$$" 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572690 1068 17592186572660 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572690 1069 "q-103326" 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572690 1070 17592186045485 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572690 1071 "Find where the tangent to the curve $y={x}^{4}-5$ at the point $(-1,-4)$ intersects the $x$-axis." 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572690 1072 #uuid "477025c6-a218-5107-ac1d-dc64cfa74878" 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572690 1074 "Let $f(x)={x}^{4}-5.$ Let's first calculate the derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{4}-5)=4{x}^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the slope of the tangent at our point is\n\n$$\nm={f}^{′}(-1)=4(-1)^{3}=-4.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the tangent is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-(-4) & =-4(x+1) \\\\ y & =-4x-4-4 \\\\ y & =-4x-8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find where the tangent line intercepts the $x$-axis, we set $y=0$ and solve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0 & =-4x-8 \\\\ x & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point of intersection is $(-2,0).$" 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572691 1068 17592186572608 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572691 1069 "q-115639" 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572691 1070 17592186045485 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572691 1071 "![](images/9e/9e3a9bc6a2c642697cfa2b6525e3abf42a0227f0ac66eecade156a842fb5e92d.png)\n\nThe graph above shows a direct variation between the variables $u$ and $z.$ Which equation describes the relationship between the variables?" 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572691 1072 #uuid "528c6dbc-6dbe-5499-88b0-b6881a756455" 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572691 1074 "Since $u$ varies directly with $z,$ we use the equation\n\n$$\nu=kz.\n$$\n\nNext, we notice there is a point on the graph with $z=-10$ and $u=2.$ So, we substitute this data into the equation to find $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2 & =k\\cdot (-10) \\\\ -\\frac{2}{10} & =k \\\\ -\\frac{1}{5} & =k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the direct variation equation is given by\n\n$$\nu=-\\frac{1}{5}z.\n$$" 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572692 1068 17592186572612 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572692 1069 "q-29060" 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572692 1070 17592186045485 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572692 1071 "Solve the inequality $-2x-8\\le 4.$" 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572692 1072 #uuid "599ba4ee-64d2-5b0c-a2ee-cad2a14843d9" 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572692 1074 "First, we apply the addition principle, adding $8$ to both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2x-8 & \\le 4 \\\\ -2x-8+8 & \\le 4+8 \\\\ -2x+0 & \\le 12 \\\\ -2x & \\le 12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we apply the multiplication principle, dividing both sides by $-2.$ \n\nRemember, when multiplying or dividing an inequality by a negative number, we need to flip the inequality sign:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2x\\,\\, & \\le \\,\\,12 \\\\ \\frac{-2x}{-2}\\, & \\ge \\,\\,\\frac{12}{-2} \\\\ \\frac{\\cancel{-2}x}{\\cancel{-2}}\\, & \\ge \\,\\,-6 \\\\ x\\,\\, & \\ge \\,-6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x\\ge -6.$" 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572693 1068 17592186572655 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572693 1069 "q-128616" 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572693 1070 17592186045487 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572693 1071 "Simplify $(A∨B)∨A.$" 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572693 1072 #uuid "5aad22c2-f4b5-5b1b-92ab-159f5eea4c65" 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572693 1074 "Using the commutative, associative, and idempotent laws, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(A∨B)∨A & ≡(B∨A)∨A \\\\  & ≡B∨(A∨A) \\\\  & ≡B∨A.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572694 1068 17592186572614 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572694 1069 "q-343898" 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572694 1070 17592186045486 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572694 1071 "![](images/15/15b37063b57cc846bfc1776c1d6ec50a2170440289da1e7482b365c197c11410.png)\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is differentiable on $[-3,3].$ The graph of ${f}^{′}(x)$ is shown above. Determine the maximum value of $f(x)$ on $[-3,3]$ if $f(-3)=10.$\n\n${f}_{\\text{max}}=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572694 1072 #uuid "63b0fa41-9cba-5e63-9815-21bb52243039" 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572694 1074 "According to the candidate test, the maximum value of $f(x)$ on $[-3,3]$ must occur at a critical point or an endpoint of the interval. \n\n**Step 1**: Find the critical points:\n\nWe see that ${f}^{′}(x)$ is defined everywhere on $x\\in (-3,3),$ and ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ at $x=1.$ Therefore, the candidates are $x=-3,1,3.$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the value of $f$ for each candidate using the fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\nThe function ${f}^{′}(x)$ is made up of a trapezoid and a triangle. Let's add the areas bounded by these shapes to our diagram:\n\n![](images/bd/bd1d4466a6a0d7f8640200c999170dff2aa2762fc2cd14a8e61c110c6e94b189.png)\n\nUsing the given information and the fundamental theorem of calculus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-3) & =10 \\\\ f(1) & =f(-3)+{\\int }_{-3}^{1}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =10-5 \\\\  & =5 \\\\ f(3) & =f(-3)+{\\int }_{-3}^{3}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =10+(-5+2) \\\\  & =7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Select the largest value of $f$ from our candidates. \n\nComparing the values of $f$ found in step $2,$ we have\n\n$$\n{f}_{\\text{max}}=max\\{10,5,7\\}=10.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the maximum value of $f$ is ${f}_{\\text{max}}=10,$ and the value of $x$ that maximizes $f(x)$ is ${x}_{\\text{max}}=-3.$" 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572695 1068 17592186572659 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572695 1069 "q-327052" 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572695 1070 17592186045487 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572695 1071 "![](images/22/22f309ffc9ff6ae4a43a5faae12e4ed952d65aec70f87a7e71f64594f0c61f92.png)\n\nConsider the picture above. If $c=\\frac{4}{3}a+\\lambda b,$ then$\\,\\lambda =$\n{{field-1}}." 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572695 1072 #uuid "63f90954-aef6-5358-9e26-c4da4dbb21fd" 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572695 1074 "To find $\\lambda ,$ we want to match the expression\n\n$$\nc=\\frac{4}{3}a+\\lambda b.\n$$\n\nWe are given that one of the components is $\\frac{4}{3}a.$ So, we first draw the vector $\\frac{4}{3}a$ from the origin. Then we complete the path to the point $C.$\n\n![](images/b8/b8db5015e22e55282e180b1d81f7a2e1ca544dfe0f468dc4dedb6283bac2da5c.png)\n\nLet $P$ be the endpoint of the vector $\\frac{4}{3}a.$ Then\n\n$$\nc=\\overset{\\to }{OP}+\\overset{\\to }{PC}.\n$$\n\nIn $△OPC,$ we have:\n\n- $PC=\\frac{1}{4}|b|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{PC}$ and $b$ have the same direction\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{PC}=\\frac{1}{4}b.$\n\nAdditionally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c & =\\overset{\\to }{OP}+\\overset{\\to }{PC} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}a+\\frac{1}{4}b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $c=\\frac{4}{3}a+\\lambda b=\\frac{4}{3}a+\\frac{1}{4}b.$\n\nSince $a∦b,$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients of $b,$ we get:\n\n$$\n\\lambda =\\frac{1}{4}.\n$$" 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572696 1068 17592186572611 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572696 1069 "q-128618" 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572696 1070 17592186045486 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572696 1071 "Simplify $(A∧A)∧(B∧A).$" 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572696 1072 #uuid "6f4a26a2-aaa2-5db2-88b4-6bb08153b831" 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572696 1074 "Using the associative, commutative, and idempotent laws, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(A∧A)∧(B∧A) & ≡A∧(B∧A) \\\\  & ≡A∧(A∧B) \\\\  & ≡(A∧A)∧B \\\\  & ≡A∧B.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572697 1068 17592186572628 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572697 1069 "q-107050" 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572697 1070 17592186045485 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572697 1071 "Evaluate ${lim}_{y\\to 3}\\frac{{y}^{3}-9y}{{y}^{2}-5y+6}.$" 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572697 1072 #uuid "7acf7423-e8b7-5b28-a3d4-100be04f8153" 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572697 1074 "If we attempt to evaluate the limit, we get an indeterminate form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{y\\to 3}\\frac{{y}^{3}-9y}{{y}^{2}-5y+6} & =\\frac{{3}^{3}-9(3)}{{3}^{2}-5(3)+6}=\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, to find the limit, we have to factor the numerator and the denominator. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{y}^{3}-9y}{{y}^{2}-5y+6} & =\\frac{y({y}^{2}-9)}{(y-2)(y-3)} \\\\  & =\\frac{y(y-3)(y+3)}{(y-2)(y-3)} \\\\  & =\\frac{y(y+3)}{(y-2)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{y\\to 3}\\frac{{y}^{3}-9y}{{y}^{2}-5y+6} & ={lim}_{y\\to 3}\\frac{y(y+3)}{y-2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3(3+3)}{3-2} \\\\  & =18.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572698 1068 17592186572595 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572698 1069 "q-71022" 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572698 1070 17592186045485 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572698 1071 "Consider the straight line with the parametric equations\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=-t \\\\ y &=-3-2t \\\\ z &=4+7t\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nfor $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ Find the Cartesian equation of this line." 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572698 1072 #uuid "7e38002f-06be-5b6a-8718-39abfb36add8" 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572698 1074 "First, let's find the corresponding vector equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-t \\\\ -3-2t \\\\ 4+7t\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}-t \\\\ -2t \\\\ 7t\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}+t\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ r & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}+t\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\nThis implies that the given line passes through the point $P(0,-3,4)$ and is parallel to the vector $v=⟨-1,-2,7⟩.$ So, its Cartesian (canonical) equation is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-0}{-1}=\\frac{y-(-3)}{-2}=\\frac{z-4}{7}=t,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nSimplifying, we reach\n\n$$\n\\frac{x}{-1}=\\frac{y+3}{-2}=\\frac{z-4}{7}.\n$$" 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572699 1068 17592186572633 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572699 1069 "q-83008" 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572699 1070 17592186045487 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572699 1071 "Simplify the following sentence: \"The watch is a wristwatch, or it is a pocket watch or it is a wristwatch.\"" 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572699 1072 #uuid "7fde2761-bb61-54c2-b335-1663af74408b" 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572699 1074 "Using the commutative law, we can replace the phrase \"it is a pocket watch or it is a wristwatch\" with the phrase \"it is a wristwatch or it is a pocket watch.\" The sentence now reads\n\n\"The watch is a wristwatch, or it is a wristwatch or it is a pocket watch.\"\n\nThen, using the associative law, we can move the comma:\n\n\"The watch is a wristwatch or it is a wristwatch, or it is a pocket watch.\"\n\nNow, we can simplify the phrase \"The watch is a wristwatch or it is a wristwatch\" to just \"The watch is a wristwatch.\" The sentence now reads\n\n\"The watch is a wristwatch, or it is a pocket watch.\"\n\nFinally, we can write the sentence more concisely:\n\n\"The watch is a wristwatch or a pocket watch.\"" 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572700 1068 17592186572593 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572700 1069 "q-42470" 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572700 1070 17592186045485 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572700 1071 "Find $6det(A)-2det(B)$, where \n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 7 & 3 & 2 \\\\ \\sqrt{3} & 1 & 2 & 9 \\\\ 1 & 7 & 3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 5\\end{bmatrix},\\,B=\\begin{bmatrix}3\\sqrt{2} & 3 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572700 1072 #uuid "7fe1aa9d-37dc-5179-a9aa-2b200892c342" 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572700 1074 "Let's examine each matrix in turn.\n\n- Since $A$ has two identical rows, we obtain\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 7 & 3 & 2 \\\\ \\sqrt{3} & 1 & 2 & 9 \\\\ 1 & 7 & 3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 5\\end{bmatrix}\\,⟹\\,det(A)=0.\n$$\n\n- Since $B$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix, its determinant is:\n\n$$\ndet(B)=3\\sqrt{2}\\cdot 0-1\\cdot 3=-3\n$$\n\nTherefore, $6det(A)-2det(B)=6\\cdot 0-2\\cdot (-3)=6.$" 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572701 1068 17592186572641 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572701 1069 "q-81715" 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572701 1070 17592186045485 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572701 1071 "Which of the following are logically equivalent to $P?$\n\nI. $P∧(Q∨P)$\n\nII. $Q∧(P∨Q)$\n\nIII. $(Q∨P)∧P$" 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572701 1072 #uuid "82b62b9d-dd4f-549e-842a-634acfc5109f" 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572701 1074 "The law that conjunction absorbs disjunction is stated as\n\n$$\nP∧(P∨Q)≡P.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each of the given statements.\n\n- Statement I is logically equivalent to $P.$ We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P∧(Q∨P) & ≡P∧(P∨Q) \\\\  & ≡P.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is *not* logically equivalent to $P.$ We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q∧(P∨Q) & ≡Q∧(Q∨P) \\\\  & ≡Q≢P.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is logically equivalent to $P.$ We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(Q∨P)∧P & ≡P∧(P∨Q) \\\\  & ≡P.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572702 1068 17592186572594 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572702 1069 "q-38949" 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572702 1070 17592186045487 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572702 1071 "Given that $(x+1)$ is a factor of $f(x)={x}^{3}+{x}^{2}+3x+3,$ which of the following curves is generated by $y=f(x)?$\n\n(*Note: Figures not drawn to scale*)" 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572702 1072 #uuid "88a63990-6594-5760-b73c-c8f3ff85a424" 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572702 1074 "First, we need to factor the polynomial. We are told that $(x+1)$ is a factor of $f(x),$ so $(x+1)$ divides $f(x)$ with zero remainder. To begin factoring $f(x),$ we can divide it by $(x+1)$ using synthetic division:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & {x}^{3} & {x}^{2} & {x}^{1} & {x}^{0} \\\\ -1 & 1 & 1 & 3 & 3 \\\\  &  & -1 & 0 & -3 \\\\  & 1 & 0 & 3 & 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{f(x)}{x+1}={x}^{2}+3\n$$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\nf(x)=(x+1)({x}^{2}+3).\n$$\n\nThe equation is now fully factored. Note that the discriminant of ${x}^{2}+3=0$ is \n\n$$\nD={0}^{2}-4(1)(3)=-12<0,\n$$\n\nwhich implies that the quadratic factor has no real roots. So by the zero-product rule, we deduce that $f(x)$ has a single root at $x=-1.$ \n\nWe also find the $y$-intercept of the curve by substituting the value $x=0$ into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & ={0}^{3}+{0}^{2}+3(0)+3=3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the curve:\n\n- has a $y$-intercept at $(0,3),$\n\n- passes through the $x$-axis at the single root $x=-1,$ and\n\n- has no more $x$-intercepts.\n\nBecause the leading coefficient is positive, the graph takes an inverted $\\text{S}$-shape. Therefore, the graph looks as follows:\n\n![](images/6f/6f9f2ce252975ee7c375071a74256b1e3a8822fbea8c2787af20f566a495102a.png)" 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572703 1068 17592186572603 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572703 1068 17592186572631 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572703 1069 "q-320766" 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572703 1070 17592186045486 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572703 1071 "Write $\\frac{12{x}^{7}}{6{x}^{7}+4}$ in the form $a+\\frac{b}{6{x}^{7}+4},$ where $a\\ne 0.$\n\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"1\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"25\" width=\"30\">$12{x}^{7}$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" rowspan=\"2\" width=\"25\">$=$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" rowspan=\"2\" width=\"30\">\n{{field-1}}\n</td>\n<td align=\"center\" rowspan=\"2\" width=\"25\">$+$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"25\" width=\"60\">\n{{field-2}}\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"25\" width=\"30\">$6{x}^{7}+4$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"25\" width=\"60\">$6{x}^{7}+4$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572703 1072 #uuid "902313f6-5fff-5e8d-b85b-2153357455ee" 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572703 1074 "First, we factor the numerator to put $6{x}^{7}$ in parentheses. Then, we add and subtract $4$ in the parentheses. This reveals a copy of the denominator without changing the value of the expression.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{12{x}^{7}}{6{x}^{7}+4} & =\\frac{2(6{x}^{7})}{6{x}^{7}+4} \\\\  & =\\frac{2(6{x}^{7}+4-4)}{6{x}^{7}+4} \\\\  & =\\frac{2[(6{x}^{7}+4)-(4)]}{6{x}^{7}+4} \\\\  & =\\frac{2(6{x}^{7}+4)-2(4)}{6{x}^{7}+4} \\\\  & =\\frac{2(6{x}^{7}+4)-8}{6{x}^{7}+4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we split the fraction into two, and then simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2(6{x}^{7}+4)-8}{6{x}^{7}+4} & =\\frac{2(6{x}^{7}+4)}{6{x}^{7}+4}+\\frac{-8}{6{x}^{7}+4} \\\\  & =2+\\frac{-8}{6{x}^{7}+4}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572704 1068 17592186572589 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572704 1069 "q-70144" 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572704 1070 17592186045487 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572704 1071 "![](images/69/695c825905b268e40b7c77c681c608fcedfed4d0c5150f76224c4e60eb8ab045.png)\n\nConsider the picture above. Given that $c=\\lambda a+2b$, find $\\lambda .$" 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572704 1072 #uuid "95085e88-c005-5feb-bf13-36ae5ea5d897" 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572704 1074 "To find $\\lambda ,$ we want to match the expression\n\n$$\nc=\\lambda a+2b.\n$$\n\nWe are given that one of the components is $2b.$ So, we first draw the vector $2b$ from the origin. Then we complete the path to the point $C.$\n\n![](images/7d/7d388cdd1db5cfe59b447c3fc27b597fc4e42397966935592f56c1c9f4a598e6.png)\n\nLet $P$ be the endpoint of the vector $2b.$ Then\n\n$$\nc=\\overset{\\to }{OP}+\\overset{\\to }{PC}.\n$$\n\nIn $△OPC,$ we have:\n\n- $PC=|a|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{PC}$ and $a$ have the same direction\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{PC}=a.$\n\nAdditionally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c & =\\overset{\\to }{OP}+\\overset{\\to }{PC} \\\\  & =2b+a.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $c=\\lambda a+2b=a+2b.$\n\nSince $a∦b,$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients of $a,$ we get:\n\n$$\n\\lambda =1\n$$" 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572705 1068 17592186572600 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572705 1069 "q-82995" 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572705 1070 17592186045485 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572705 1071 "Simplify the sentence \"The house is pretty, or it is pretty or white.\"" 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572705 1072 #uuid "9609c9d7-0f30-5ae1-9971-3c66a1bfd653" 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572705 1074 "First, let's rewrite the phrase \"it is pretty or white\" as the equivalent phrase \"it is pretty or it is white.\" The sentence now reads\n\n\"The house is pretty, or it is pretty or it is white.\"\n\nThen, using the associative law, we can move the comma:\n\n\"The house is pretty or it is pretty, or it is white.\"\n\nNow, we can simplify the phrase \"the house is pretty or it is pretty\" to just \"the house is pretty.\" The sentence now reads\n\n\"The house is pretty, or it is white.\"\n\nFinally, we can write the sentence more concisely as\n\n\"The house is pretty or white.\"" 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572706 1068 17592186572607 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572706 1069 "q-102542" 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572706 1070 17592186045487 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572706 1071 "Find the root of $f(x)=-3\\sqrt[7]{8x+11}+3.$" 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572706 1072 #uuid "98b881b2-8a42-522a-b7c6-2baeb5b35a53" 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572706 1074 "To find the root of $f(x),$  we must solve $f(x)=0:$\n\n$$\n-3\\sqrt[7]{8x+11}+3=0\n$$\n\nWe solve this equation by making $x$ the subject, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3\\sqrt[7]{8x+11}+3 & =0 \\\\ -3\\sqrt[7]{8x+11} & =-3 \\\\ \\sqrt[7]{8x+11} & =1 \\\\ (\\sqrt[7]{8x+11})^{7} & ={1}^{7} \\\\ 8x+11 & =1 \\\\ 8x & =-10 \\\\ x & =-\\frac{5}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the root of $f(x)$ is $x=-\\frac{5}{4}.$" 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572707 1068 17592186572657 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572707 1069 "q-83006" 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572707 1070 17592186045487 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572707 1071 "Simplify the following sentence: \"The day is rainy and it is cold, and the day is rainy.\"" 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572707 1072 #uuid "ac648f3a-f305-5539-9491-09df3a7946e8" 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572707 1074 "Using the commutative law, we can replace the phrase \"The day is rainy and it is cold\" with the phrase \"The day is cold and it is rainy.\" The sentence now reads\n\n\"The day is cold and it is rainy, and the day is rainy.\"\n\nThen, using the associative law, we can move the comma:\n\n\"The day is cold, and it is rainy and the day is rainy.\"\n\nNow, we can simplify the phrase \"it is rainy and the day is rainy\" to just \"it is rainy.\" The sentence now reads\n\n\"The day is cold, and it is rainy.\"\n\nFinally, we can write the sentence more concisely:\n\n\"The day is cold and rainy.\"" 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572708 1068 17592186572610 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572708 1069 "q-120273" 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572708 1070 17592186045487 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572708 1071 "![](images/5d/5dea155246de4f35697780db7ebf1802cdf5c2899fe42fa494993be2f65dd7f3.png)\n\nConsider the picture above. Given that $h=\\lambda f+\\mu g$, find $\\lambda$ and $\\mu .$" 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572708 1072 #uuid "ada3d5e7-07f2-5981-9fcd-59fe51195895" 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572708 1074 "![](images/99/9905448ad59cb2f68f5def832811eb34056305d1a535f23387f4403a340cb8f8.png)\n\nIn $△OFH$ shown above, we have \n\n- $OF=\\frac{1}{2}|f|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{OF}$ has the opposite direction to $f$\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{OF}=-\\frac{1}{2}f.$\n\nSimilarly:\n\n- $FH=2|g|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{FH}$ has the opposite direction to $g$\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{FH}=-2g.$\n\nAdditionally, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}h & =\\overset{\\to }{OF}+\\overset{\\to }{FH} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}f-2g.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $h=\\lambda f+\\mu g=-\\frac{1}{2}f-2g.$\n\nSince $f∦g,$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients, we get:\n\n- $\\lambda =-\\frac{1}{2}$ and $\\mu =-2$" 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572709 1068 17592186572591 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572709 1069 "q-93094" 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572709 1070 17592186045487 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572709 1071 "Find the root of $f(x)=\\sqrt[4]{5x+16}-3.$" 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572709 1072 #uuid "ada425c5-b3e7-5d8d-b301-db69b1684794" 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572709 1074 "To find the root of $f(x),$  we must solve $f(x)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\sqrt[4]{5x+16}-3=0\n$$\n\nWe solve this equation by making $x$ the subject, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt[4]{5x+16}-3 & =0 \\\\ \\sqrt[4]{5x+16} & =3 \\\\ (\\sqrt[4]{5x+16})^{4} & ={3}^{4} \\\\ 5x+16 & =81 \\\\ 5x & =65 \\\\ x & =13\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we must check the answer by substituting it back into the initial equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt[4]{5\\cdot 13+16}-3 & =0 \\\\ \\sqrt[4]{81}-3 & =0 \\\\ 0 & =0\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the root of $f(x)$ is $x=13.$" 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572710 1068 17592186572598 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572710 1069 "q-316970" 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572710 1070 17592186045487 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572710 1071 "![](images/99/994e49d2360232104932da5a7798e1621071676ccad9015908303aafc36c7258.png)\n\nThe function $g(x)$ is defined and differentiable on $[-2,4].$ The graph of ${g}^{′}(x),$ the derivative of $g(x),$ is shown above, where the areas of the regions between the graph and the $x$-axis are $3$ and $5$ respectively (from left to right). Find the maximum value of $g(x)$ on the interval $[-2,4]$ given that $g(-2)=1.$\n\n${g}_{\\text{max}}=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572710 1072 #uuid "b47a3384-43e3-5364-9f00-98abf9eb16af" 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572710 1074 "According to the candidate test, the maximum value of $g(x)$ on $[-2,4]$ must occur at a critical point or an endpoint of the interval.\n\n**Step 1**: Find the critical points:\n\nWe see that ${g}^{′}(x)$ is defined everywhere on $x\\in (-2,4),$ and ${g}^{′}(x)=0$ at $x=1.$ Therefore, the candidates are $x=-2,1,4.$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the value of $g$ for each candidate using the fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\nUsing the given information and the fundamental theorem of calculus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(-2) & =1 \\\\ g(1) & =g(-2)+{\\int }_{-2}^{1}{g}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =1+3 \\\\  & =4 \\\\ g(4) & =g(-2)+{\\int }_{-2}^{4}{g}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =1+(3-5) \\\\  & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Select the largest value of $g$ from our candidates. \n\nComparing the values of $g$ found in step $2,$ we have\n\n$$\n{g}_{\\text{max}}=max\\{1,4,-1\\}=4.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the maximum value of $g$ is ${g}_{\\text{max}}=4,$ and the value of $x$ that maximizes $g(x)$ is ${x}_{\\text{max}}=1.$" 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572711 1068 17592186572609 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572711 1069 "q-340294" 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572711 1070 17592186045485 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572711 1071 "Given that $f(x)=\\frac{9x-3}{2x}$, find ${f}^{′}(x).$\n\n${f}^{′}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572711 1072 #uuid "b512e4a8-0dc7-5d1e-b726-5bdd6894a13e" 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572711 1074 "The numerator and denominator are $u(x)=9x-3$ and $v(x)=2x,$ respectively. Their derivatives are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′}(x) & =9, \\\\ {v}^{′}(x) & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the quotient rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{{u}^{′}(x)\\cdot v(x)-u(x)\\cdot {v}^{′}(x)}{[v(x)]^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{9\\cdot 2x-(9x-3)\\cdot 2}{{(2x)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{18x-18x+6}{4{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{4{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2{x}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572712 1068 17592186572652 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572712 1069 "q-83016" 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572712 1070 17592186045487 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572712 1071 "Simplify $P∨(Q∨P).$" 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572712 1072 #uuid "b8b24239-40f1-5136-9956-57abae0c726e" 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572712 1074 "Using the commutative, associative, and idempotent laws, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P∨(Q∨P) & ≡P∨(P∨Q) \\\\  & ≡(P∨P)∨Q \\\\  & ≡P∨Q.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572713 1068 17592186572638 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572713 1069 "q-39041" 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572713 1070 17592186045486 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572713 1071 "Given that $x$ is a factor of $f(x)=-{x}^{3}-2{x}^{2}-4x,$ which of the following curves is generated by $y=f(x)?$\n\n(*Note: Figures not drawn to scale*)" 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572713 1072 #uuid "bec25d7a-3c2c-5bfa-ad41-b6aabdf70b0c" 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572713 1074 "First, let's factor the polynomial. We factor out a common factor $-x,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =-{x}^{3}-2{x}^{2}-4x \\\\  & =-x({x}^{2}+2x+4)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe equation is now fully factored. Note that the discriminant of ${x}^{2}+2x+4=0$ is \n\n$$\nD={2}^{2}-4(1)(4)=-12<0,\n$$\n\nwhich implies that the quadratic factor has no real roots. So by the zero-product rule, we deduce that $f(x)$ has a single root at $x=0.$ \n\nWe also find the $y$-intercept of the curve by substituting the value $x=0$ into the equation:\n\n$$\ny=-{0}^{3}-2(0)^{2}-4(0)=0\n$$\n\nSo, the curve:\n\n- has a $y$-intercept at $(0,0),$\n\n- passes through the $x$-axis at the single root $x=0,$ and\n\n- has no more $x$-intercepts.\n\nBecause the leading coefficient is negative, the graph has an $\\text{S}$-shape. Therefore, the graph looks as follows:\n\n![](images/f6/f621534a395f8b0692d31bb3b491b0237877e6dc3ea94a2299d354857c7c3bfd.png)" 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572714 1068 17592186572622 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572714 1069 "q-343709" 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572714 1070 17592186045486 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572714 1071 "![](images/b3/b3fcbed98f7838b4885e2ca14cb1192c039e0b1d35e5980cca363ec4cdc6f9f5.png)\n\nThe function $w(x)$ is defined and differentiable on $[-6,3].$ The graph of ${w}^{′}(x),$ the derivative of $w(x),$ is shown above, where the areas of the regions between the graph and the $x$-axis are $7,$ $5,$ and $1$ respectively (from left to right). Find the maximum value of $w(x)$ on the interval $[-6,3]$ given that $w(-6)=-4.$\n\n${w}_{\\text{max}}=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572714 1072 #uuid "c20cc6cd-9570-529a-a16b-801a9c33c8af" 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572714 1074 "According to the candidate test, the maximum value of $w(x)$ on $[-6,3]$ must occur at a critical point or an endpoint of the interval. \n\n**Step 1**: Find the critical points:\n\nWe see that ${w}^{′}(x)$ is defined everywhere on $x\\in (-6,3),$ and ${w}^{′}(x)=0$ at $x=-2,1.$ Therefore, the candidates are $x=-6,-2,1,3.$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the value of $w$ for each candidate using the fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\nUsing the given information and the fundamental theorem of calculus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}w(-6) & =-4 \\\\ w(-2) & =w(-6)+{\\int }_{-6}^{-2}{w}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-4+7 \\\\  & =3 \\\\ w(1) & =w(-6)+{\\int }_{-6}^{1}{w}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-4+(7-5) \\\\  & =-2 \\\\ w(3) & =w(-6)+{\\int }_{-6}^{3}{w}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-4+(7-5+1) \\\\  & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Select the largest value of $w$ from our candidates. \n\nComparing the values of $w$ found in step $2,$ we have\n\n$$\n{w}_{\\text{max}}=max\\{-4,3,-2,-1\\}=3.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the maximum value of $w$ is ${w}_{\\text{max}}=3,$ and the value of $x$ that maximizes $w(x)$ is ${x}_{\\text{max}}=-2.$" 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572715 1068 17592186572619 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572715 1069 "q-280713" 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572715 1070 17592186045485 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572715 1071 "Compute the volume $V$ of the parallelepiped spanned by the vectors $x=⟨2,3,0⟩$, $y=⟨1,4,5⟩$, and $z=⟨0,2,6⟩.$\n\n$V=${{field-1}}" 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572715 1072 #uuid "c885246f-c4a6-5da3-b8cb-28ee3dc779d8" 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572715 1074 "The volume of a parallelepiped spanned by three vectors is given by the absolute value of their triple product.\n\nFirst, we compute the triple product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x\\cdot (y\\times z) & =\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 4 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 6\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}4 & 5 \\\\ 2 & 6\\end{vmatrix}-3\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 6\\end{vmatrix}+0\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2(4\\cdot 6-5\\cdot 2)-3(1\\cdot 6-5\\cdot 0)+0 \\\\  & =2(24-10)-3(6) \\\\  & =28-18 \\\\  & =10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we take the absolute value. So, the volume of the corresponding parallelepiped is \n\n$$\nV=|10|=10.\n$$" 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572716 1068 17592186572635 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572716 1068 17592186572646 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572716 1069 "q-277079" 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572716 1070 17592186045485 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572716 1071 "Suppose we are given two vectors $a$ and $b$ where $a∦b.$ Find $\\lambda$ and $\\mu ,$ if \n\n$$\na-2b=(3\\lambda -\\mu )a-(\\lambda -4\\mu )b.\n$$\n\n$\\lambda =$\n{{field-1}}$,\\,\\mu =$\n{{field-2}}" 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572716 1072 #uuid "d2a5cac6-33f2-5ce9-8655-67db2ef94b7e" 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572716 1074 "Since $a∦b,$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. So, the coefficients of $a$ must be equal, and the coefficients of $b$ must be equal.\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}3\\lambda -\\mu &=1 \\\\ \\lambda -4\\mu &=2\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFrom the first equation, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\mu =3\\lambda -1\n$$\n\nSubstituting back into the second equation, we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lambda -4\\mu  & =2 \\\\ \\lambda -4(3\\lambda -1) & =2 \\\\ \\lambda -12\\lambda +4 & =2 \\\\ -11\\lambda  & =-2 \\\\ \\lambda  & =\\frac{2}{11}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mu  & =3\\lambda -1 \\\\  & =3\\cdot \\frac{2}{11}-1 \\\\  & =-\\frac{5}{11}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572717 1068 17592186572606 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572717 1069 "q-83011" 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572717 1070 17592186045487 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572717 1071 "Simplify $(A∨A)∧(A∧B).$" 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572717 1072 #uuid "d4c8e41b-9e14-5786-91ae-524e6c4c4e4d" 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572717 1074 "Using the associative and idempotent laws, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(A∨A)∧(A∧B) & ≡A∧(A∧B) \\\\  & ≡(A∧A)∧B \\\\  & ≡A∧B.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572718 1068 17592186572661 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572718 1069 "q-89251" 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572718 1070 17592186045485 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572718 1071 "Simplify the sentence \"The dress is red and elegant, and it is elegant.\"" 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572718 1072 #uuid "d9600c9e-6c82-5b6e-b3f2-54ca64b173d3" 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572718 1074 "First, let's rewrite the phrase \"The dress is red and elegant\" as the equivalent phrase \"The dress is red and the dress is elegant.\" The sentence now reads\n\n\"The dress is red and the dress is elegant, and it is elegant.\"\n\nThen, using the associative law, we can move the comma:\n\n\"The dress is red, and the dress is elegant and it is elegant.\"\n\nNow, we can simplify the phrase \"the dress is elegant and it is elegant\" to just \"the dress is elegant.\" The sentence now reads\n\n\"The dress is red, and the dress is elegant.\"\n\nFinally, we can write the sentence more concisely as\n\n\"The dress is red and elegant.\"" 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572719 1068 17592186572615 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572719 1069 "q-68193" 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572719 1070 17592186045486 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572719 1071 "![](images/25/2501f7e346abf6c79a211fd090162be685c001bc6acc68787d8c73c0eee7a8af.png)\n\nThe graph above shows a proportional relationship between the weight $x$ (in pounds) of ice cream sold by a store and the profit $y$ (in dollars).  What is the constant of proportionality?" 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572719 1072 #uuid "d9eeaf2b-c92c-529e-9218-24a01a5754ee" 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572719 1074 "To compute the constant of proportionality, we first need to choose a point $(x,y)$ on the graph. Here, let's choose the point $(3,4).$\n\n![](images/7b/7beacc5836ed16a4dcb36916d0273513b7b2d94871c4ee5994d56479bc11f18d.png)\n\nNow, we can find the constant of proportionality by computing the ratio of the $y$-coordinate to the $x$-coordinate, as follows:\n\n$$\nk=\\frac{y}{x}=\\frac{4}{3}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write the resulting improper fraction as a mixed number:\n\n$$\n\\frac{4}{3}=1\\,\\text{R}1=1\\,\\frac{1}{3}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the constant of proportionality is $1\\,\\frac{1}{3}$ dollars per pound." 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572720 1068 17592186572617 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572720 1068 17592186572634 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572720 1069 "q-319887" 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572720 1070 17592186045487 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572720 1071 "Write $\\frac{7{x}^{2}+11}{7{x}^{2}-x}$ in the form $a+\\frac{bx+c}{7{x}^{2}-x},$ where $a\\ne 0.$\n\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"1\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"25\" width=\"20\">$7{x}^{2}+11$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" rowspan=\"2\" width=\"20\">$=$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" rowspan=\"2\" width=\"20\">\n{{field-1}}\n</td>\n<td align=\"center\" rowspan=\"2\" width=\"20\">$+$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"25\" width=\"20\">\n{{field-2}} \n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"25\" width=\"20\">$7{x}^{2}-x$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"25\" width=\"25\">$7{x}^{2}-x$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572720 1072 #uuid "e60ce661-ee8f-513d-978f-4505c3b6113e" 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572720 1074 "First, we add and subtract $x$ in the numerator. This reveals a copy of the denominator without changing the value of the expression.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{7{x}^{2}+11}{7{x}^{2}-x}\\, &=\\,\\frac{7{x}^{2}-x+x+11}{7{x}^{2}-x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we split the fraction into two, and then simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{7{x}^{2}-x+x+11}{7{x}^{2}-x} & =\\frac{7{x}^{2}-x}{7{x}^{2}-x}+\\frac{x+11}{7{x}^{2}-x} \\\\  & =1+\\frac{x+11}{7{x}^{2}-x}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572721 1068 17592186572613 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572721 1069 "q-11964" 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572721 1070 17592186045485 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572721 1071 "Given that $f(x)=\\frac{5{x}^{2}}{9x-7}$, find ${f}^{′}(x).$" 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572721 1072 #uuid "e77736b2-0ba9-5e43-b9dd-36b855b6cec7" 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572721 1074 "The numerator and denominator are $u(x)=5{x}^{2}$ and $v(x)=9x-7,$ respectively. Their derivatives are given by\n\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′}(x) & =10x, \\\\ {v}^{′}(x) & =9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{{u}^{′}(x)\\cdot v(x)-u(x){v}^{′}(x)}{[v(x)]^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{10x\\cdot (9x-7)-(5{x}^{2})\\cdot 9}{{(9x-7)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{90{x}^{2}-70x-45{x}^{2}}{{(9x-7)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{45{x}^{2}-70x}{{(9x-7)}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572722 1068 17592186572632 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572722 1069 "q-77509" 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572722 1070 17592186045486 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572722 1071 "![](images/66/667454b0cdd06afe7b722aad8ad64423491e8e13a3eb950945af7bedcb7291c9.png)\n\nGiven the vectors $a$ and $b$ shown above, which of the following could be the linear combination $-4a-3b?$" 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572722 1072 #uuid "e8943fb5-5821-50a1-aa29-5b9a2bc234c4" 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572722 1074 "First, recall that\n\n$$\n-4a-3b=-4a+(-3b).\n$$\n\n- The vector $-4a$ has the direction that is opposite to $a$ and $4$ times the magnitude.\n\n- The vector $-b$ has the direction that is opposite to $b$ and $3$ times the magnitude.\n\n![](images/fb/fb02d818d2ad88acc13802287bc02fa194afba38ec018429b867fe5f21a6206e.png)\n\nNow, we can draw $-3b$ starting from the head of $-4a.$ The resultant \n\n$$\n-4a-3b\n$$\n\ngoes from the tail of $-4a$ to the head of $-3b.$\n\n![](images/e3/e30a16feb7f1fcc75789443db72de1fda10b61762401d19ee296df11aa03b09d.png)" 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572723 1068 17592186572642 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572723 1069 "q-51883" 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572723 1070 17592186045487 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572723 1071 "![](images/fa/fa895816cc537fc2ccb30f99f2f9c8ec01cf0ee75ba76fc8a2aed1c0b3349206.png)\n\nThe graph above shows that the volume of gas $L$ in a truck's tank is directly proportional to the time $t$ since the driver started filling it. Find a direct variation equation that describes the relationship between $L$ and $t.$" 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572723 1072 #uuid "eabcc9c8-1fae-5b8f-9d65-4f29f12ab5ce" 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572723 1074 "Since $L$ varies directly with $t,$ we use the equation\n\n$$\nL=kt.\n$$\n\nNext, we notice that there is a point on the graph with $t=6$ and $L=90.$ So, we substitute this data into the equation to find $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}90 & =k\\cdot 6 \\\\ \\frac{90}{6} & =k \\\\ 15 & =k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the direct variation equation is given by\n\n$$\nL=15t.\n$$" 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572724 1068 17592186572605 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572724 1069 "q-103065" 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572724 1070 17592186045485 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572724 1071 "Which of the following are logically equivalent to $M?$\n\nI. $(M∧L)∧M$\n\nII. $M∧(M∨L)$\n\nIII. $(L∨M)∧M$" 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572724 1072 #uuid "eb08b876-0b66-567e-a3b3-60f7e35a27a7" 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572724 1074 "The law that conjunction absorbs disjunction is stated as\n\n$$\nM∧(M∨L)≡M.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each of the given statements.\n\n- Statement I is *not*  logically equivalent to $M.$\nThis statement cannot be simplified using the absorption laws.\n\n- Statement II is logically equivalent to $M.$ We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M∧(M∨L) & ≡M.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is logically equivalent to $M.$ We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(L∨M)∧M & ≡M∧(L∨M) \\\\  & ≡M∧(M∨L) \\\\  & ≡M.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572725 1068 17592186572623 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572725 1069 "q-120265" 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572725 1070 17592186045487 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572725 1071 "![](images/67/6715bda9432c2ba3e61c31f436cd6b7608312da499bb4372944d25db6cf76090.png)\n\nConsider the picture above. Given that $c=\\lambda a+\\mu b$, find $\\lambda$ and $\\mu .$" 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572725 1072 #uuid "eeb1828d-7e99-5d6a-aa95-0e286af84c7f" 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572725 1074 "![](images/89/8997fda665e4bba248dd19f5b8fe0d2a2524df341042271a0f8426e0ce152ecd.png)\n\nIn $△OAC$ shown above, we have:\n\n- $OA=3|a|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{OA}$ and $a$ have opposite directions\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{OA}=-3a.$\n\nSimilarly:\n\n- $AC=2|b|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{AC}$ and $b$ have opposite directions\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{AC}=-2b.$\n\nAdditionally, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c & =\\overset{\\to }{OA}+\\overset{\\to }{AC} \\\\  & =-3a-2b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $c=\\lambda a+\\mu b=-3a-2b.$ \n\nSince $a∦b,$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients, we get:\n\n- $\\lambda =-3$ and $\\mu =-2$" 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572726 1068 17592186572599 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572726 1069 "q-280746" 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572726 1070 17592186045487 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572726 1071 "Find $k$ given that the vectors $a=⟨k,k,2⟩$, $b=⟨1,3,0⟩$, and $c=⟨0,4,5⟩$ are coplanar.\n\n$k=${{field-1}}" 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572726 1072 #uuid "ef51444c-d286-591b-9ba9-aedca70d62d8" 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572726 1074 "Computing the triple product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot (b\\times c) & =\\begin{vmatrix}k & k & 2 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 5\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =k\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}3 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 5\\end{vmatrix}-k\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{vmatrix}+2\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =k(3\\cdot 5-0\\cdot 4)-k(1\\cdot 5-0\\cdot 0)+2(1\\cdot 4-3\\cdot 0) \\\\  & =k(15)-k(5)+2(4) \\\\  & =15k-5k+8 \\\\  & =10k+8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThree vectors are coplanar if and only if their triple product is zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot (b\\times c) & =0 \\\\ 10k+8 & =0 \\\\ 10k & =-8 \\\\ k & =-\\frac{4}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the three vectors are coplanar if and only if $k=-\\frac{4}{5}.$" 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572727 1068 17592186572651 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572727 1069 "q-28199" 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572727 1070 17592186045485 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572727 1071 "Which of the following curves is generated by $y=f(x)$ where $f(x)=(3-x)(1-x)^{2}?$" 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572727 1072 #uuid "f860d929-2f10-587c-a6b0-b33c9769a489" 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572727 1074 "The equation is already in factored form, so by the zero-product rule, we deduce that it has a single root at $x=3$ and a double root at $x=1.$ \n\nWe also find the $y$-intercept of the curve by substituting the value $x=0$ into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =(3-0)(1-0)^{2} \\\\  & =(3)(1)^{2} \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the curve\n\n- intercepts the $y$-axis at the point $(0,3),$\n\n- passes through the $x$-axis at the single root $x=3,$ and\n\n- touches the $x$-axis and turns around at the double root $x=1.$\n\nNotice that the leading coefficient is *negative*:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =(3-x)(1-x)^{2} \\\\  & =(3-x)({x}^{2}-2x+1) \\\\  & =-{x}^{3}+⋯\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n![](images/20/20ba88abb40a98b3d832402627568a1536abde65018e09cce9c89e184128132b.png)" 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572728 1068 17592186572618 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572728 1069 "q-48388" 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572728 1070 17592186045485 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572728 1071 "Evaluate ${lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{9{x}^{3}-4x}{3{x}^{2}+2x}.$" 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572728 1072 #uuid "f8c786dd-8e95-5f38-8555-c19baa5a85a4" 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572728 1074 "If we attempt to evaluate the limit, we get an indeterminate form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{9{x}^{3}-4x}{3{x}^{2}+2x} & =\\frac{9(0)^{3}-4(0)}{3(0)^{2}+2(0)}=\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, to find the limit, we have to factor the numerator and the denominator. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{9{x}^{3}-4x}{3{x}^{2}+2x} & =\\frac{x(9{x}^{2}-4)}{x(3x+2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{9{x}^{2}-4}{3x+2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{9{x}^{3}-4x}{3{x}^{2}+2x} & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{9{x}^{2}-4}{3x+2} \\\\  & =\\frac{0-4}{0+2} \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572729 1068 17592186572597 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572729 1069 "q-89270" 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572729 1070 17592186045487 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572729 1071 "Simplify $(Q∧P)∧Q.$" 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572729 1072 #uuid "fd1f6b88-5ede-50c3-bec3-63756a7c54a9" 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572729 1074 "Using the commutative, associative, and idempotent laws, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(Q∧P)∧Q & ≡(P∧Q)∧Q \\\\  & ≡P∧(Q∧Q) \\\\  & ≡P∧Q.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572730 1058 17592186572745 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572730 1062 #uuid "05e1162b-9ac7-5dd4-9211-b622aacaf22f" 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572730 1063 17592186125053 13194139536428 175921860454